<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaki, Azerbaijan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shaki, Nukha, Sheki</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sheki is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> on the southern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-caucasus">Greater Caucasus</a>, long a stop on regional trade routes and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> production. From the 1740s it was the capital of the Sheki Khanate, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-the-shaki-khans">Palace of the Shaki Khans</a>, decorated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stained-glass">stained glass</a> and wall paintings, survives. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> annexed the khanate in 1819, and the historic centre is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shaki-azerbaijan.jpg" alt="Shaki, Azerbaijan" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sefer azeri · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%9E%C9%99ki_%C5%9F%C9%99h%C9%99ri_%C3%BCmumi_g%C3%B6r%C3%BCn%C3%BC%C5%9F._%C3%A7ayk%C9%99nar%C4%B1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Shaki lies in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> on the southern side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-caucasus">Greater Caucasus</a>, about 240 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, surrounded by peaks that in places reach 3,000 to 3,600 metres. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> and Gurjhana are its main rivers. The city was known as Nukha until 1968. Azerbaijani historians trace its name to the Sakas, an Iranian people who reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a> in the 7th century BCE, and traces of large settlements date back more than 2,700 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Shaki was one of the larger cities of the Caucasian Albanian states in the 1st century. After an Arab invasion in 654 it was annexed to the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of Arminiya, and around the turn of the 9th century Armenian Smbatean princes ruled it as an autonomous principality, Shaki or Hereti. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiurikian-dynasty">Kiurikian dynasty</a> ruled it from 1038 to 1105, and in 1117 King David IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> captured it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> took it in 1392. The Safavid shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> conquered the area in the early 1500s, and in 1551 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Shah Tahmasp</a> appointed the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> governor. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> briefly held it twice.</p><p>In 1743 Haji Chelebi, who claimed descent from the earlier hereditary rulers, proclaimed an independent Sheki Khanate. Nadir Shah sent an army against him, but after Nadir's death Haji Chelebi declared himself khan again. The khanate raised silkworms and traded, and it defeated the army of Heraclius II of Kakheti in 1751. By the treaty of Kyurekchay of 21 May 1805 it agreed to annexation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which formally took the area by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan">Treaty of Gulistan</a> in 1813. The khanate was abolished in 1819 and the Shaki province established in its place.</p><p>In the mid-19th century the city became an international <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> centre, and more than 200 European companies opened offices there. A rising against collectivisation broke out in a village of the district in 1930 and was put down.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Because the town was devastated many times, its oldest surviving monuments date only from the 16th to 19th centuries. Among them is the Palace of Shaki Khans, built in 1762 without a single nail as a summer residence and decorated with shebeke, wooden lattices of coloured glass held without glue or nails. Others are the Shaki fortress, the Khan's Mosque and two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>. The historic centre with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai-palace">Khan's Palace</a> was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Heritage List in 2019. Shaki has its own dialect of Azerbaijani and is known for sweet dishes such as Shaki halva.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shaki, Azerbaijan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its rulers balanced between the Safavid Empire, the khanates of <b>Shaki</b> and Shirvan, the Lezgin free communities of Jar and Belokan and later Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/elisu-sultanate">Elisu Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Agha Mohammad Khan's Qajar Iran took control of the region in the 1790s; Jafar Qoli Khan fled north in 1799 and was later installed by Russia as khan of <b>Sheki</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-khoy">Khanate of Khoy</a></li><li><blockquote>Hereti lay along the Alazani and Iori rivers and around <b>Shaki</b>, a borderland between Kartli and the old Caucasian Albania.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hereti">Principality of Hereti</a></li><li><blockquote>He made <b>Shaki</b> an independent khanate, one of the first of the Caucasian khanates, and after a mudflow destroyed old Shaki the capital moved to Nukha.</blockquote><a href="/state/shaki-khanate">Shaki Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
